WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.320
A science experiment on groups&nbsp;
of boys from competing camps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.840
who have no idea they’re being manipulated into&nbsp;
fighting each other — what was that all about?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.780
In the summer of 1954, a school bus with a&nbsp;
group of 11- and 12-year old boys arrived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:28.080
at Robbers Cave State Park in Oklahoma. What&nbsp;
the kids didn’t know: there was another bus.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.720
All the boys thought they were&nbsp;
going camping. Their parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.140
at home believed the sponsored trip&nbsp;
had something to do with leadership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.460
None of them knew that the camp&nbsp;
staff were actually researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.000
and the boys’ part of a controversial experiment.&nbsp;
One of its goals: let the boys fight each other.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.480
The Robbers Cave Experiment was&nbsp;
designed to unfold in three stages:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.100
in-group formation, friction,&nbsp;
and conflict resolution.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.600
It started with "in-group formation".&nbsp;
In the first week each group did various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.900
activities without interacting with another.&nbsp;
The boys had fun, went swimming and did hikes.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.520
Over time social norms developed.&nbsp;
Leaders and followers emerged. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.420
boys formed friendships and started&nbsp;
inventing their own subculture.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.460
Eventually they came up with names. One group&nbsp;
called themselves the eagles and the others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:39.060
chose to be the rattlers. They stenciled their&nbsp;
names onto shirts and flags. The in-groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.980
were formed. The tribes had an identity.&nbsp;
They were now ready to meet each other.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.460
During the "friction phase", the two&nbsp;
groups came into contact with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:56.400
other. The researchers set up competitions with&nbsp;
single prizes for the winners. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.420
they established a limited resource&nbsp;
for the two to battle over.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.120
It didn’t take long before the&nbsp;
in-groups developed negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.760
attitudes towards the outgroup. Prejudice&nbsp;
became apparent. To make matters worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.980
the staff suggested to one group, to dump&nbsp;
buckets of mud inside the cabin of the others.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.740
Eventually the kids grew hateful, violent,&nbsp;
and verbally abusive. They burned each other's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.900
flags and sometimes things escalated to the&nbsp;
point that the camp staff had to step in.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.780
Last was conflict resolution. The boys were meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.060
to make peace. First the staff tried to&nbsp;
reduce the prejudice between the groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:47.160
by increasing contact and communication.&nbsp;
But that just made matters worse.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.960
Then the researchers blocked the valve to&nbsp;
the camp’s water tank. As there was no more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.140
drinking water, the boys became progressively&nbsp;
thirstier. Then the camp staff suggested that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:07.740
they all needed to collaborate to fix the problem.&nbsp;
Reluctantly, the groups started to get to work.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:16.740
Before long the boys were mixing and cooperating.&nbsp;
There were no Rattlers or Eagles any more, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:23.400
a bunch of campers collaborating. When the water&nbsp;
finally came through, there was common rejoicing.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.360
The researchers learned four&nbsp;
key things from the experiment.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.880
First: Individual differences are not&nbsp;
responsible for tribal conflicts. Age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:42.900
race, culture or religion don’t seem to&nbsp;
matter. Second, hostile attitudes arise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:50.220
when groups compete for resources that only one&nbsp;
of them can get. Third, discussions don’t solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:57.360
conflicts. Fourth, only a common goal or enemy&nbsp;
that promotes cooperation reduces the friction.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.820
Two renowned psychologists, Muzafer Sherif&nbsp;
and his wife Carolyn Wood Sherif were behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.600
the controversial experiment&nbsp;
and later established a theory.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.640
Realistic conflict theory explains how&nbsp;
hostility arises as a result of competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:25.560
for limited resources, such as money, power,&nbsp;
military protection, or social status. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.480
these resources are actually limited or&nbsp;
just perceived to be so, doesn’t matter.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:38.040
Their work helps us understand the mechanisms&nbsp;
of discrimination against outsiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.520
which escalates during shortages. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.520
when in-groups think that good jobs are hard&nbsp;
to find, they often try to remove sources of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.520
out-group competition, lobby for legal&nbsp;
restrictions, or deny newcomers access.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.500
What do you think? Do tribal conflicts&nbsp;
only arise as a result of competition for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:08.400
scarce goods? And if so, are shared goals and&nbsp;
common enemies really the only way to peace?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.240
Or is the theory flawed and not&nbsp;
applicable to adults? After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.320
children are easier to influence&nbsp;
— not only to build a prejudice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:25.260
but also to change their ideas again later.&nbsp;
